Suggest from a composition only when it typed, and end the session at a touch
elsewhere on Android.

SuggestModel ignores the caret and the text while the editor composes, and
when a composition ends it arms a quick suggestion, or refilters an open
list, as if the word had just been typed. A keyboard can compose a word it
did not type. Gboard 12.4 underlines the word under a tap, or under the
caret after Home or an accepted suggestion, by rewriting it with itself,
which moves no caret. When that composition ended, because the keyboard was
put away with Back or the hide key, the list opened over the tapped word
(`b|ee` gave `bean`, `beat`; `d|og` gave `door`, `dot`), and after a Tab
accept it could open again with the accepted word as its only row. A
composition now counts as typed when the last caret move during it was
typing (an empty selection, reason NotSet, source 'keyboard' or
'deleteLeft'), and one that typed nothing ends without arming anything. The
end of a composition also checks the character before the caret against the
language's trigger characters, and a composition that typed nothing typed
none, so that check is skipped too. That half follows from the code rather
than from a failure seen: in the published bundle a tap at the start of
`alpha2`, after a space, recomposed the word and Back then opened no list,
the JavaScript provider offering nothing for a space there, and Gboard did
not recompose the word after a period at all. A composition that typed
nothing still refilters an open list when it ends, as before. A desktop
input method moves the caret with every key of a composition, so it is
unaffected.

A touch on another part of the workbench, an activity bar icon or a status
bar item, leaves focus in the editor on Android, because the workbench's
touch gestures cancel the events that would move it, so the blur that ends
a session on a desktop never comes. A list stayed open over the view the
touch had opened, and the next tap there accepted a suggestion into the
file: on an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4, `hello world` became `hello
WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream` and `ki` became `kite` from taps meant for
files in the Explorer, which did not open. The page's keyboard guard
(MainActivity.injectKeyboardGuard) puts the keyboard away at such a touch,
which ends a word still underlined, and the end of that composition arms a
quick suggestion, which needs only the editor to keep focus (read from the
code), so the list would open over the new view. On Android a
pointerdown outside the editor and its overflow widgets now ends the session
as that blur does, a request still out or a quick suggestion still pending
included, and the composition the keyboard then ends counts as one that
typed nothing, so its end arms nothing either. What that costs: a touch
outside the editor closes the list wherever it lands, a scroll of another
view included, as a click there does on a desktop.

Verified with the tree built from these patches, on an API 33 emulator with
Gboard 12.4 and an API 37 emulator with Gboard 17.7.4. On API 33 a tap in
`b|ee` followed by Back opened no list. With `alp` underlined on an empty
line of a JavaScript file, a tap on the Explorer icon put the keyboard away,
ended the word and opened no list, and a tap on a file in the Explorer
opened that file; after a typed `alp` and Back the list opened with the
keyboard down, as before, and the tap on the Explorer icon closed it at the
pointerdown. On API 37, with the list open over a typed `alp`, the same tap
closed it, and a tap on a file in the tree opened the file. Each time the
file held what was typed.

